 record. It follows the model a QUERY_CONFIG / WRITE_CONFIG names by index
 (HmiRequest.DurationMs: 0 = the active model, n = AvailableModels[n]).]]></Comment><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>12328</BitOffs></SubItem></DataType><DataType><Name Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_ConfigPage</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §3.10.2 — one bounded page of the configuration manifest, published in
 ST_HmiResponse. The HMI requests QUERY_CONFIG with IntValue = PageIndex and
 walks 0..PageCount-1; Revision lets it detect a changed manifest (activation,
 changeover, config write) and refetch.]]></Comment><BitSize>197760</BitSize><SubItem><Name>Revision</Name><Type>UDINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize><BitOffs>0</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>PageIndex</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>32</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>PageCount</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>48</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>EntryCount</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>64</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>Entries</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_ConfigEntry</Type><ArrayInfo><LBound>1</LBound><Elements>16</Elements></ArrayInfo><BitSize>197632</BitSize><BitOffs>128</BitOffs></SubItem></DataType><DataType><Name Namespace="Fraktal_Core">FB_ConfigPager</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §3.10.2 — windowed writer for the configuration manifest. QUERY_CONFIG
 re-walks the whole export enumeration each request; the pager counts every
 appended entry and stores only the requested page window into the published
 ST_ConfigPage, so no full manifest buffer ever exists in PLC memory and the
 walk order alone defines the paging. Exporters call M_Append*/ignore paging.

 §3.8b — it is ALSO the sink for a parameter-set save, and that is the whole
 point of putting it here. A set is "a projection produced by the same
 deterministic walk that builds the §3.10.2 manifest, keyed per value by
 (Scope, WriteKey)". Making the set a second walk would be a second authority
 on what a station's configuration consists of, and the two would drift the
 first time a module registered a value (§1.1 O9). So M_SetupSetSave puts the
 pager in record mode, the ordinary M_AppendConfig walk runs unchanged, and
 every capability it emits is offered to the store. No exporter knows which
 mode it is feeding, and none can be written to feed only one of them.]]></Comment><BitSize>512</BitSize><SubItem><Name>_pTarget</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core" PointerTo="1">ST_ConfigPage</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize><BitOffs>64</BitOffs><Properties><Property><Name>OPC.UA.DA</Name><Value>0</Value></Property></Properties></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_cursor</Name><Type>DINT</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ entries appended so far in this walk]]></Comment><BitSize>32</BitSize><BitOffs>128</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_first</Name><Type>DINT</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ first cursor index stored in the window (0-based)]]></Comment><BitSize>32</BitSize><BitOffs>160</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_storedSlot</Name><Type>INT</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ 1-based slot written by the last M_Append; 0 if absent]]></Comment><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>192</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_setStore</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">I_ConfigStore</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b set-save mode. Exactly one of _pTarget / _setStore is ever live.]]></Comment><BitSize>64</BitSize><BitOffs>256</BitOffs><Properties><Property><Name>OPC.UA.DA</Name><Value>0</Value></Property></Properties></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_setKind</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ConfigKind</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize><BitOffs>320</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_setCount</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>352</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_setFailed</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>368</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_pAccess</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core" PointerTo="1">FB_AccessManager</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8d - the root's access manager, set before a manifest walk. 0 (a pager
 nobody configured, such as a test's) publishes every value as readable.]]></Comment><BitSize>64</BitSize><BitOffs>384</BitOffs><Properties><Property><Name>OPC.UA.DA</Name><Value>0</Value></Property></Properties></SubItem><SubItem><Name>_modelOnly</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8a - another model's page: only model-scoped capabilities are counted
 and stored. Reset by every M_Setup.]]></Comment><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>448</BitOffs></SubItem><Method><Name>M_FinishSetSave</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b — close the set save. FALSE means nothing was stored: an empty set, a
 record that would not fit, or a store that refused the commit. The previous
 set of that name is left exactly as it was.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Records</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize><Properties><Property><Name>ItemType</Name><Value>Output</Value></Property></Properties></Parameter><Local><Name>store</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">I_ConfigStore</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>_M_OfferRecord</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b — offer one capability to an open set save. Called from
 M_AppendCapability, so the set contains exactly what the manifest publishes
 as editable and nothing else: no unregistered value can enter a set, and no
 registered one can be forgotten by it.

 Only records of the set's own ConfigKind are taken — a station set carries
 STATION_CFG, a model set carries PAR_CFG (§3.8b "one mechanism, two kinds").
 Anything that will not fit a record FAILS the save rather than shortening a
 value: a set that quietly lost a character restores a machine to almost its
 commissioned state, which is worse than not restoring it.]]></Comment><Parameter><Name>Scope</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>ValueText</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>WriteKey</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>WriteRevision</Name><Type>UDINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>ConfigKind</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ConfigKind</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>ValueType</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ConfigValueType</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Writable</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter><Local><Name>record</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_ConfigRecord</Type><BitSize>2720</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>M_AppendNumber</Name><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Scope</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Item</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Number</Name><Type>DINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>M_SetAccess</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8d - the access manager whose policy and session decide what the next
 manifest walk may show. Set by the root before each QUERY_CONFIG.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>pAccess</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core" PointerTo="1">FB_AccessManager</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>M_Setup</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Begin one QUERY_CONFIG walk targeting the published page window.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Target</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core" PointerTo="1">ST_ConfigPage</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>PageIndex</Name><Type>INT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Revision</Name><Type>UDINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>M_Append</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Append one manifest entry; stored only when it falls inside the page window.
 Always returns TRUE so exporters can chain without branching.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Scope</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Item</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>ValueText</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>M_SetModelOnly</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8a - the rest of this walk serves another model's record: only
 capabilities marked ModelScoped are counted and stored, so the answer is that
 model's data - a page or two - not the whole station manifest.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Enabled</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>M_Finish</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Close the walk: publish the total page count for the HMI's fetch loop.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize></Method><Method><Name>_M_Window</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Count one entry of the walk and store it when it falls in the page window.]]></Comment><Parameter><Name>Scope</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Item</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>ValueText</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Local><Name>slot</Name><Type>DINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Local></Method><Method><Name>M_SetupSetSave</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b — begin a parameter-set save. The caller then runs the ordinary
 M_AppendConfig walk and closes with M_FinishSetSave.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Store</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">I_ConfigStore</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Info</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_ConfigSetInfo</Type><BitSize>2016</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>M_AppendCapability</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Append one typed configuration-write capability. All inputs are explicit for
 TwinCAT 4024 compatibility. The owning handler remains authoritative.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Scope</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Item</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>ValueText</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>WriteKey</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>WriteRevision</Name><Type>UDINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>ConfigKind</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ConfigKind</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>ValueType</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ConfigValueType</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Writable</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>RequiresReady</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>HasMinimum</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>HasMaximum</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Minimum</Name><Type>LREAL</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Maximum</Name><Type>LREAL</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Unit</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_EngUnit</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>LabelKey</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>EnumDomain</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>EnumLabelKey</Name><Type>STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>648</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>ClassId</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8d; '' = built-in class]]></Comment><Type>STRING(40)</Type><BitSize>328</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>MinReadLevel</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_AccessLevel</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>MinWriteLevel</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_AccessLevel</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>ModelScoped</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core 0.19 - follows the model a request names]]></Comment><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter><Local><Name>readLevel</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_AccessLevel</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>writeLevel</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_AccessLevel</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Local><Local><Name>readable</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Local></Method><Properties><Property><Name>PouType</Name><Value>FunctionBlock</Value></Property></Properties></DataType><DataType><Name Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_ConfigAccess</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §3.8d - what a module declared about who may see and change
 one of its registered values. The ROOT turns this into an effective level
 with its policy; the module never decides policy, it only states the class
 and its own raise-only minimums.]]></Comment><BitSize>448</BitSize><SubItem><Name>Kind</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ConfigKind</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize><BitOffs>0</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>ClassId</Name><Type>STRING(40)</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ '' = the built-in class (DATA_READ/DATA_WRITE)]]></Comment><BitSize>328</BitSize><BitOffs>32</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>MinReadLevel</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_AccessLevel</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ raise-only: never lowers the class level]]></Comment><BitSize>32</BitSize><BitOffs>384</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>MinWriteLevel</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_AccessLevel</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize><BitOffs>416</BitOffs></SubItem></DataType><DataType><Name Namespace="Fraktal_Core">I_ConfigSource</Name><BitSize>64</BitSize><ExtendsType>IQueryInterface</ExtendsType><Method><Name>M_AppendConfig</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Append this module's (and its children's) manifest entries to the pager.
 Deterministic order across calls — the walk order defines the paging.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Pager</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core" ReferenceTo="true">FB_ConfigPager</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>M_ApplyCapture</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8c - capture: store this module's OWN live value into the registered
 field named by WriteKey. No TextValue: the whole point is that the number
 comes from the machine, not from the client. Everything else - revision,
 RequiresReady, the type's own invariants, range, domain, storage - is the
 write path unchanged, so a capture can never accept what a typed write
 would refuse.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Scope</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>WriteKey</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Revision</Name><Type>UDINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>RootReady</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>M_ApplyConfigWrite</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Apply one registered configuration write to the module named by Scope.
 TRUE only from the module that owns the key and accepted the value; a module
 that does not own it returns FALSE so the walk continues past it.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Scope</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>WriteKey</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Revision</Name><Type>UDINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>TextValue</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>RootReady</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>M_ConfigAccessOf</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8d - report what the module named by Scope declared about access to one
 of its registered values. TRUE only from the owner; the root resolves the
 effective level with its policy before it lets a write, capture or set
 record reach the value.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Scope</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>WriteKey</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Grant</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core" ReferenceTo="true">ST_ConfigAccess</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize><Properties><Property><Name>ItemType</Name><Value>InOut</Value></Property></Properties></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>M_StageConfigWrite</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b — validate one write WITHOUT applying it. Same module, same key lookup,
 same revision check, same RequiresReady rule, same type-specific invariant
 hook, same range and domain checks as M_ApplyConfigWrite — and then nothing
 is stored.

 This exists because a parameter-set load is staged and all-or-nothing: every
 record must be proven acceptable BEFORE any value changes, since a
 half-applied machine parametrization is never a valid outcome. A separate
 validation predicate would be free to drift from the write path; this one
 shares the single validator with it, so "would be accepted" and "was
 accepted" cannot disagree.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Scope</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>WriteKey</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Revision</Name><Type>UDINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>TextValue</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>RootReady</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>M_TakeRestoreLoss</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8b — hand the root ONE not-yet-reported configuration restore loss from
 this module or its children, so the root can annunciate it with the owning
 module's canonical path and apply the deployment's E_ConfigRestorePolicy.

 The module marks each loss reported as it hands it over, so the root simply
 drains this until it returns FALSE. That is what makes the annunciation
 exactly-once without either side keeping a list.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Path</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ canonical identity of the module that lost it]]></Comment><Type>STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>648</BitSize><Properties><Property><Name>ItemType</Name><Value>Output</Value></Property></Properties></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Expected</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ schema version this build understands]]></Comment><Type>UINT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize><Properties><Property><Name>ItemType</Name><Value>Output</Value></Property></Properties></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Found</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ schema version actually retained]]></Comment><Type>UINT</Type><BitSize>16</BitSize><Properties><Property><Name>ItemType</Name><Value>Output</Value></Property></Properties></Parameter></Method><Properties><Property><Name>TcPlcInterfaceType</Name></Property><Property><Name>TcPlcInterfaceId</Name><Value>1455</Value></Property></Properties></DataType><DataType><Name Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_ManualEnableStatus</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §9 — "is a safety-rated enabling device permitting manual motion right
 now?", derived from the §9 device rows and never authored.

 This is STATUS, not authority. §9 is read-only to the application and the PLC
 never infers "safe" from it: the safety system decides whether guards may be
 open, enforces the reduced speed, and mutes the interlock. What this record
 says is only what the certified system has already published about its
 enabling device — so that a release (§7.6) can be written against a fact with
 a provenance, instead of against a boolean a station assembled from standard
 I/O.

 A three-position enabling device reads as permitting ONLY in its middle
 position. Released and panic-squeezed are both non-permitting, which is the
 entire reason the device has three positions rather than two.]]></Comment><BitSize>664</BitSize><SubItem><Name>Present</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ at least one enabling device is mirrored here]]></Comment><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>0</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>Held</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ one is permitting NOW - safety-evaluated, standard PLC cannot set]]></Comment><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>8</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>DeviceName</Name><Type>STRING(80)</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ which one, so a release report can name it (§7.6.0)]]></Comment><BitSize>648</BitSize><BitOffs>16</BitOffs></SubItem></DataType><DataType><Name Namespace="Fraktal_Core">I_ManualTarget</Name><BitSize>64</BitSize><ExtendsType>IQueryInterface</ExtendsType><Method><Name>M_ApplyHeld</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ §7.6.1a — apply one HELD manual request (hold-to-run) to the module named by
 Scope, carrying the enabling-device status the root derived from its §9
 safety facet.

 Enable travels WITH the request rather than being read by the module, because
 the fact belongs to the safety system and the root is where it is mirrored.
 A module that does not implement held commands inherits FB_ModuleBase's
 refusal, so an unsupported hold is a defined FALSE and never a silent no-op.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Scope</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Value</Name><Type>DINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Enable</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_ManualEnableStatus</Type><BitSize>664</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Method><Name>M_ApplyManual</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Apply one one-shot manual command to the module named by Scope. TRUE only
 from the module that owns the path AND accepted the value, so the walk
 continues past every module that is not the target. The owning module still
 validates the command against its own published catalogue (§5.6) — this
 carries the request, it does not privilege it.]]></Comment><ReturnType>BOOL</ReturnType><ReturnBitSize>8</ReturnBitSize><Parameter><Name>Scope</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><BitSize>2048</BitSize></Parameter><Parameter><Name>Value</Name><Type>DINT</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize></Parameter></Method><Properties><Property><Name>TcPlcInterfaceType</Name></Property><Property><Name>TcPlcInterfaceId</Name><Value>1603</Value></Property></Properties></DataType><DataType><Name Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ConfigStorage</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §3.8b — the PLC type actually behind a registered editable value.
 E_ConfigValueType says how the HMI renders and validates a value; this says
 how the framework parses and stores it. They are different questions: a
 settle time and a cycle count both publish as a number, and writing one into
 the other's storage would be a silent corruption rather than a rejection.
 Local to the binding (never transported), so this enum may be reordered.]]></Comment><BitSize>32</BitSize><BaseType>DINT</BaseType><EnumInfo><Text>NONE</Text><Enum>0</Enum><Comment><![CDATA[ unregistered — fails closed]]></Comment></EnumInfo><EnumInfo><Text>AS_BOOL</Text><Enum>1</Enum></EnumInfo><EnumInfo><Text>AS_INT</Text><Enum>2</Enum></EnumInfo><EnumInfo><Text>AS_DINT</Text><Enum>3</Enum></EnumInfo><EnumInfo><Text>AS_UDINT</Text><Enum>4</Enum></EnumInfo><EnumInfo><Text>AS_REAL</Text><Enum>5</Enum></EnumInfo><EnumInfo><Text>AS_LREAL</Text><Enum>6</Enum></EnumInfo><EnumInfo><Text>AS_TIME</Text><Enum>7</Enum><Comment><![CDATA[ serialized as whole milliseconds, per the HMI number contract]]></Comment></EnumInfo><EnumInfo><Text>AS_STRING</Text><Enum>8</Enum><Comment><![CDATA[ STRING(80)]]></Comment></EnumInfo><Properties><Property><Name>qualified_only</Name></Property></Properties></DataType><DataType><Name Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_ConfigWriteDef</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §3.8b/§3.10.2 — one editable value a module has registered about itself.
 The module declares it once at Setup; the framework then both PUBLISHES the
 capability (M_AppendConfig) and APPLIES writes to it (M_ApplyConfigWrite) from
 this single record, so the metadata a client validates against and the target
 the PLC writes can never drift apart (§1.1 O1/O9).

 pTarget is a pointer held in ordinary VAR — never inside a persistent
 declaration, where TwinCAT would retain an address that means nothing after a
 restart (TC3 §3.8b).]]></Comment><BitSize>8384</BitSize><SubItem><Name>Key</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.10.2 WriteKey — the mutation identity]]></Comment><BitSize>1288</BitSize><BitOffs>0</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>Item</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ browse/display fragment; never the write identity]]></Comment><BitSize>2048</BitSize><BitOffs>1288</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>Revision</Name><Type>UDINT</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ non-zero; bump when the value's meaning changes]]></Comment><BitSize>32</BitSize><BitOffs>3360</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>Kind</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ConfigKind</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize><BitOffs>3392</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>ValueType</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ConfigValueType</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize><BitOffs>3424</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>Storage</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ConfigStorage</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize><BitOffs>3456</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>pTarget</Name><Type GUID="{18071995-0000-0000-0000-000000000018}">PVOID</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize><BitOffs>3520</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>RequiresReady</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ write refused unless the owning root is READY]]></Comment><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>3584</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>HasMinimum</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>3592</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>HasMaximum</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>3600</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>Minimum</Name><Type>LREAL</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize><BitOffs>3648</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>Maximum</Name><Type>LREAL</Type><BitSize>64</BitSize><BitOffs>3712</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>Unit</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_EngUnit</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ a code; the HMI owns the symbol (std.unit.*)]]></Comment><BitSize>32</BitSize><BitOffs>3776</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>LabelKey</Name><Type>STRING(160)</Type><BitSize>1288</BitSize><BitOffs>3808</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>MaxLength</Name><Type>UINT</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ AS_STRING only: the target's declared capacity]]></Comment><BitSize>16</BitSize><BitOffs>5104</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>EnumDomain</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ pipe-separated exact accepted values ('' = none)]]></Comment><BitSize>2048</BitSize><BitOffs>5120</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>EnumLabelKey</Name><Type>STRING(80)</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ catalog prefix: value v is labelled '<key>.<v>']]></Comment><BitSize>648</BitSize><BitOffs>7168</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>pCapture</Name><Type GUID="{18071995-0000-0000-0000-000000000018}">PVOID</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8c teach capture. The live source lives on the SAME record as the value
 it writes, so a capture cannot reach a field that is not independently
 editable - the back door §3.8c(a) forbids is structurally impossible here
 rather than prevented by a check somebody could forget.
 0 = this value offers no capture, which is the default and most values.]]></Comment><BitSize>64</BitSize><BitOffs>7872</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>CaptureStorage</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_ConfigStorage</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ how to READ the source (may differ from Storage)]]></Comment><BitSize>32</BitSize><BitOffs>7936</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>ClassId</Name><Type>STRING(40)</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ §3.8d - the access axis. '' + NONE/NONE is the built-in class, which is
 exactly the behaviour before data classes existed.]]></Comment><BitSize>328</BitSize><BitOffs>7968</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>MinReadLevel</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_AccessLevel</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize><BitOffs>8320</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>MinWriteLevel</Name><Type Namespace="Fraktal_Core">E_AccessLevel</Type><BitSize>32</BitSize><BitOffs>8352</BitOffs></SubItem></DataType><DataType><Name Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_Nameplate</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §3.10.1 — digital nameplate (asset identity). Field set aligned with the
 IDTA 02006 Digital Nameplate submodel so the AAS projection (Annex K) is direct.
 Static identity: set once at Setup, never runtime-mutated, never HMI-writable.]]></Comment><BitSize>6720</BitSize><SubItem><Name>ProductUri</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ globally unique asset URI (IDTA: URIOfTheProduct)]]></Comment><BitSize>2048</BitSize><BitOffs>0</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>ManufacturerName</Name><Type>STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>648</BitSize><BitOffs>2048</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>ProductDesignation</Name><Type>STRING(80)</Type><BitSize>648</BitSize><BitOffs>2696</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>SerialNumber</Name><Type>STRING(40)</Type><BitSize>328</BitSize><BitOffs>3344</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>YearOfConstruction</Name><Type>STRING(8)</Type><BitSize>72</BitSize><BitOffs>3672</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>HardwareVersion</Name><Type>STRING(24)</Type><BitSize>200</BitSize><BitOffs>3744</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>FirmwareVersion</Name><Type>STRING(24)</Type><BitSize>200</BitSize><BitOffs>3944</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>SoftwareVersion</Name><Type>STRING(24)</Type><BitSize>200</BitSize><BitOffs>4144</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>OrderCode</Name><Type>STRING(40)</Type><BitSize>328</BitSize><BitOffs>4344</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>DocumentationUrl</Name><Type>STRING(255)</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ manual / handover docs (cf. IDTA 02004)]]></Comment><BitSize>2048</BitSize><BitOffs>4672</BitOffs></SubItem></DataType><DataType><Name Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_StateFlag</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §3.12 — one published DERIVED state flag.

 The distinction it enforces: `OutCmd` is what a command PRODUCED, latched
 until the next command; a state flag is what is TRUE RIGHT NOW, recomputed
 from the modules underneath every scan. `Homed` is the worked example - as a
 command result it keeps claiming "homed" after an operator jogs an axis off
 home in MANUAL, because no sequence runs to clear the latch. As a derived flag
 it simply stops being true.]]></Comment><BitSize>1056</BitSize><SubItem><Name>Key</Name><Type>STRING(120)</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ localization key; '' = slot never published]]></Comment><BitSize>968</BitSize><BitOffs>0</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>Value</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>968</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>Since</Name><Type>DATE_AND_TIME</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ When Value last changed, on the synchronized clock (§2.7). This is what a
 bare assignment cannot give and the operator always asks for: not just
 "is the door closed" but "how long has it been closed".]]></Comment><BitSize>32</BitSize><BitOffs>992</BitOffs></SubItem><SubItem><Name>Stale</Name><Type>BOOL</Type><Comment><![CDATA[ The owner stopped publishing this flag - it was computed behind an IF that
 stopped being true, or the code that produced it was removed. Value is
 forced FALSE: a flag nobody is computing is not a claim (§5.6 fail-safe).]]></Comment><BitSize>8</BitSize><BitOffs>1024</BitOffs></SubItem></DataType><DataType><Name Namespace="Fraktal_Core">ST_ModuleStatus</Name><Comment><![CDATA[ Core §3.10(a')/§3.12/§6.9(a) — the DATA MIRROR every module publishes each scan.
 Properties/methods serve PLC code; this struct serves OPC UA/HMI. It is the
 framework baseline of the OutImm role: name, tier, state summary, and the live
